{
    "document": {
      "id": "doc_001",
      "title": "Classical Conceptions of the Good",
      "sections": ["sec_1", "sec_2"],
      "character_start": 0,
      "character_end": 762,
      "summary": "Aristotle frames happiness (eudaimonia) as virtuous activity, while Plato roots moral knowledge in immutable Forms. Medieval scholastics fuse these strands into a Christian synthesis, and modern commentators debate whether virtue ethics can stand apart from deep metaphysical commitments."
    },
  
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "sec_1",
        "heading": "Foundations",
        "character_start": 0,
        "character_end": 370,
        "paragraphs": ["para_001", "para_002"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sec_2",
        "heading": "Synthesis and Debate",
        "character_start": 371,
        "character_end": 762,
        "paragraphs": ["para_003", "para_004"]
      }
    ],
  
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "para_001",
        "annotations": ["ann_001", "ann_002", "ann_003"],
        "text": "In his Nicomachean Ethics, Aristotle explores the nature of happiness (eudaimonia) as the highest human good, arguing that a fulfilled life is one lived in accordance with virtue.",
        "character_start": 0,
        "character_end": 189
      },
      {
        "id": "para_002",
        "annotations": ["ann_004", "ann_005", "ann_006", "ann_007"],
        "text": "Plato, conversely, situates knowledge of the Good within the realm of immutable Forms, suggesting that the sensible world merely participates in these perfect exemplars.",
        "character_start": 190,
        "character_end": 370
      },
      {
        "id": "para_003",
        "annotations": ["ann_008", "ann_009", "ann_010"],
        "text": "Subsequent scholastic thinkers attempted to reconcile these classical positions, interweaving Aristotelian teleology with Platonic metaphysics to articulate a Christian synthesis.",
        "character_start": 371,
        "character_end": 561
      },
      {
        "id": "para_004",
        "annotations": ["ann_011", "ann_012", "ann_013", "ann_014", "ann_015"],
        "text": "Modern commentators debate whether such integrative readings obscure important tensions, questioning if virtue ethics can truly be disentangled from metaphysical commitments.",
        "character_start": 562,
        "character_end": 762
      }
    ],
  
